file storage is a critical need for many  enterprise applications and workloads  but file storage is about more than just  disk space it's about finding a solution  that is  fast consistent scalable and still  simple to use  [Music]  some of the most common use cases for  file storage in  enterprise applications are media  rendering  data analytics and managing shared  content  these complex situations require  flexible and scalable solutions  plenty of us have heard it before  storage is cheap  but with needs growing exponentially to  fit use cases like these  companies want flexibility to scale on  demand  and meet specific performance needs of  their application  and user demands and now that companies  are turning to cloud computing solutions  for applications  and for their infrastructure there's a  growing demand for cloud-based file  systems  to meet the needs of customers looking  for a fully managed cloud-based storage  product  google offers file store  file store gives users a simple native  experience for standing up managed  network attached storage  or nas for their compute engine and  google kubernetes engine instances  we also rely on file storage partners  such as netapp  and dell emc isilon today we're going to  show you how to quickly configure and  attach your file system to a compute  instance  say we have an intensive workload and  many clients and high throughput  is critical to begin i'll name my  instance  for example performance test  next i have to select a service tier  since i need a high performance  application with many connected clients  i'll go with high scale for help picking  the tier that's right for you  you can check out the comparison table  the storage type for high scale is ssd  which works for me and with basic i  would also have the option of hdd  i'll keep capacity at 60 terabytes and  can always scale up or down when needed  without any service interruption  as i specify different capacities i can  check the calculator for cost and  performance estimates  now i have to choose a location since my  clients are located in us  central 1 the default location is  perfect  i can set a specific zone or let the  deployment decide for me  next i'll select the network used by my  clients so they'll be able to access  this file system  once i have a network i'll allocate an  ip range or let the service do this for  me  the file system mount point ip address  will be selected from this range  next i'll give my share a name dcc  this will be represented in the mount  location later even though i can grant  or restrict access to my file system  through ipb-based controls  i'll leave it open for now and now i'm  ready to create my instance  once it's ready i can see all my  instance details and copy the mount  point string  now i'll ssh into a client vm i already  created and mount my file system using  the mount point i copied earlier to  slash mnt directory  let's run some loads and take a look at  the performance  i'm starting with 256 clients performing  simultaneously with intensive iops  you can see the clients are building  their working set  and here you can see the latency  leveling off at 10 milliseconds  and iops reaching 150 000 since my  clients are saturating the instance  i need higher performance and more  capacity so i'm going to scale up my  instance from 60 terabytes to 320  terabytes  after my instance scales up you can see  the graph start to change  here the latency graph drops showing  much lower latency  and the iops graph is climbing to 600  000 iops  as we'd expect the total throughput is  also growing accordingly  for anyone that's ever managed their own  storage and panicked because they needed  to slap another  physical drive in a server before they  ran out of space this was pretty  exciting  today we saw how google cloud's file  storage is used to migrate traditional  applications  modernize existing applications with  kubernetes and scale to meet the  performance demands of big compute  workloads  from media rendering to genomics  processing google's file storage can  meet the needs of business critical  workloads  thanks for joining us and please check  out our website and documentation to  learn  even more about file store
 

